                        <div id="accordions" class="accordion">
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading1" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse1">
                                        <span><strong>What is the rule of three in writing?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse1" class="collapse show">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>It's the idea that ideas, phrases, or events grouped in threes feel more complete and memorable than groups of two or four. Three is the smallest number that reads as a genuine pattern rather than a coincidence.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading2" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse2">
                                        <span><strong>What's a good rule of three example?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse2" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Classic ones include "blood, sweat and tears" and "beginning, middle, end." In Australian speechwriting, Paul Keating's Redfern Address is a widely studied example of the technique used at length.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading3" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse3">
                                        <span><strong>How do I structure a persuasive essay in Australia?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse3" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Most Australian curricula teach a three-part shape: state your claim, support it with evidence (often using the OREO scaffold, opinion, reason, example, opinion), then restate what you want the reader to think, feel, or do.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading4" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse4">
                                        <span><strong>How should I start a persuasive essay or speech?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse4" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Open with something that earns attention rather than a flat thesis statement, a short scenario, a pointed question, or a bold claim you'll go on to prove. Save the formal thesis for the sentence right after the hook.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading5" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse5">
                                        <span><strong>What is the OREO writing structure?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse5" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>It stands for opinion, reason, example, opinion. It's taught widely in Australian schools as a scaffold for persuasive paragraphs, and it works for a similar reason the rule of three does, it gives an argument a clear, repeatable shape.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading6" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse6">
                                        <span><strong>Is two or four ever better than three?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse6" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Yes. A pair can feel complete without a third element, "love and loss" is a good example, and technical or safety writing usually wants clarity over rhythm, so a fourth point should be included if it's genuinely needed rather than dropped to force a triad.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading7" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse7">
                                        <span><strong>How do I practise the rule of three without sounding formulaic?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse7" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Vary how often you use it, make sure every element in a triad adds something distinct, and read your work aloud. If a triad feels forced when spoken, it usually is.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading8" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse8">
                                        <span><strong>Does the rule of three work in business writing and pitches?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse8" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Yes, the problem-solution-benefit structure is one of the most common triads in business communication, and grouping report findings or email points into three tends to make them easier for busy readers to retain.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading9" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse9">
                                        <span><strong>Where can Australian writers learn more about the rule of three?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse9" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>The Australian Writers' Centre, Writing NSW, and Kate Forsyth's own writing journal are all solid starting points, alongside any recent Miles Franklin-winning novel if you want to see the pattern working in the wild.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="card">
                                <div id="heading10" class="card-header bg-white border-0">
                                    <div class="collapsible-link" data-target="#collapse10">
                                        <span><strong>Does the rule of three apply to fiction and non-fiction equally?</strong></span>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                                <div id="collapse10" class="collapse">
                                    <div class="card-body text">
                                        <p>Yes, though it shows up differently, three-act structure and character trios in fiction, three-part arguments and essay structures in non-fiction, and repetition-with-variation in speeches.</p>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            </div>
